WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.645
Last time, we named sodium chloride as ions,

00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:07.071
and carbon dioxide as atoms sharing electrons.

00:00:07.755 --> 00:00:09.668
But these aren't two separate worlds.

00:00:10.452 --> 00:00:13.300
Remember our tug of war from the electronegativity

00:00:13.361 --> 00:00:15.727
video, with two atoms pulling on a shared

00:00:15.768 --> 00:00:16.549
pair of electrons.

00:00:17.194 --> 00:00:19.227
If the pull is even, the pair stays

00:00:19.328 --> 00:00:19.791
in the middle.

00:00:20.456 --> 00:00:22.869
If one atom pulls harder, the pair shifts

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:23.453
toward it.

00:00:24.197 --> 00:00:26.325
And if one atom pulls much, much harder,

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:28.394
it can take the electrons completely.

00:00:29.197 --> 00:00:32.162
Let's see how that one idea connects covalent

00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:33.144
and ionic bonding.

00:00:34.148 --> 00:00:37.093
Electronegativity measures how strongly an atom in a

00:00:37.214 --> 00:00:39.138
bond attracts the shared electrons.

00:00:39.742 --> 00:00:41.693
It rises toward the top right of the

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:42.539
periodic table.

00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:45.497
Fluorine is the highest, at four point zero.

00:00:45.941 --> 00:00:47.443
Chlorine is three point two,

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:51.151
hydrogen two point two, and sodium only zero

00:00:51.212 --> 00:00:51.692
point nine.

00:00:52.336 --> 00:00:54.927
What decides the bond is the difference between

00:00:54.948 --> 00:00:56.052
the two atoms' values.

00:00:57.018 --> 00:00:58.531
Start with two chlorine atoms.

00:00:59.156 --> 00:01:01.883
Each has seven outer electrons, and needs one

00:01:01.924 --> 00:01:02.104
more.

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:04.881
So they share a pair, one electron from

00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:05.082
each.

00:01:05.727 --> 00:01:08.136
That shared pair is a covalent bond.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.183
Both atoms pull equally, because the difference is

00:01:11.244 --> 00:01:13.808
zero, so the pair sits exactly in the

00:01:13.849 --> 00:01:14.068
middle.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.758
That's a non-polar covalent bond.

00:01:17.621 --> 00:01:19.069
Now hydrogen and chlorine.

00:01:19.552 --> 00:01:21.883
The difference is three point two minus two

00:01:21.943 --> 00:01:23.772
point two, which is one point zero.

00:01:24.476 --> 00:01:27.108
Chlorine pulls harder, so the shared pair spends

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:28.876
more of its time near the chlorine.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.590
The chlorine end gets a small negative charge,

00:01:31.911 --> 00:01:34.101
and the hydrogen end a small positive charge.

00:01:34.764 --> 00:01:37.125
We write these as delta minus and delta

00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:39.847
plus, where delta means partial, a bit less

00:01:39.888 --> 00:01:40.768
than a full charge.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.218
This is a polar covalent bond.

00:01:44.111 --> 00:01:45.924
Now sodium and chlorine.

00:01:46.449 --> 00:01:48.739
The difference is three point two minus zero

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.788
point nine, which is two point three.

00:01:51.593 --> 00:01:54.339
Chlorine's pull is so much stronger that sodium's

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.484
outer electron moves across completely.

00:01:57.287 --> 00:01:59.894
Sodium becomes a plus one ion, and chlorine

00:01:59.956 --> 00:02:01.439
becomes a minus one ion.

00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:04.676
These ions attract each other strongly, because opposite

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:05.661
charges attract.

00:02:06.185 --> 00:02:08.221
That attraction is an ionic bond.

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:11.756
So bonding is a spectrum, not two boxes.

00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:14.624
As the difference grows, the shared pair moves

00:02:14.705 --> 00:02:17.210
further toward one atom, until it's handed over.

00:02:18.053 --> 00:02:20.298
As a rough guide, a difference below about

00:02:20.359 --> 00:02:22.824
zero point four is non-polar covalent.

00:02:23.346 --> 00:02:25.432
Between zero point four and one point seven

00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:26.616
is polar covalent.

00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:29.495
And above about one point seven is ionic.

00:02:30.262 --> 00:02:31.634
You can see it on the periodic table,

00:02:31.675 --> 00:02:31.856
too.

00:02:32.381 --> 00:02:33.903
A metal from the bottom left, with a

00:02:33.984 --> 00:02:36.409
non-metal from the top right, are far apart

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.934
in electronegativity, so they bond ionically.

00:02:39.677 --> 00:02:42.225
Two non-metals, close together, share electrons.

00:02:42.982 --> 00:02:43.567
Quick prediction.

00:02:44.191 --> 00:02:46.717
In hydrogen fluoride, the difference is four point

00:02:46.737 --> 00:02:48.902
zero minus two point two, which is one

00:02:48.942 --> 00:02:49.383
point eight.

00:02:50.146 --> 00:02:51.635
By the guide, that's ionic.

00:02:52.358 --> 00:02:54.728
So is hydrogen fluoride made of ions?

00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:55.821
Have a think.

00:02:59.915 --> 00:03:00.442
It isn't.

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:03.880
Hydrogen fluoride is made of separate molecules, and

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:05.909
it boils at about twenty degrees Celsius.

00:03:06.552 --> 00:03:09.359
An ionic solid like salt doesn't melt until

00:03:09.561 --> 00:03:10.463
eight hundred and one.

00:03:11.266 --> 00:03:13.814
So its bond is strongly polar, but still

00:03:13.895 --> 00:03:14.456
covalent.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.715
That's why these numbers are only a guide.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.321
Bonds near the boundary have some of both

00:03:19.402 --> 00:03:19.861
characters,

00:03:20.403 --> 00:03:22.884
so in an exam, say about one point

00:03:22.925 --> 00:03:24.205
seven, and use the evidence.

00:03:25.128 --> 00:03:27.814
Chemists show these electron moves with Lewis dot

00:03:27.855 --> 00:03:28.375
diagrams.

00:03:29.018 --> 00:03:31.865
Each dot is one outer electron, drawn around

00:03:31.906 --> 00:03:32.707
the element's symbol.

00:03:33.511 --> 00:03:35.881
Inner electrons aren't shown, because only the outer

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.247
ones take part in bonding.

00:03:37.971 --> 00:03:40.778
For sodium chloride, sodium has one dot, and

00:03:40.839 --> 00:03:41.880
chlorine has seven.

00:03:42.543 --> 00:03:44.519
The dot moves from sodium to chlorine.

00:03:45.185 --> 00:03:47.457
Sodium is left with no dots, and chlorine

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:48.322
now has eight.

00:03:49.086 --> 00:03:51.252
We put each ion in square brackets, with

00:03:51.313 --> 00:03:52.415
its charge outside.

00:03:53.102 --> 00:03:55.058
Magnesium chloride works the same way.

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:59.116
Magnesium has two outer electrons, and each chlorine

00:03:59.156 --> 00:04:00.120
can take only one.

00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:03.449
So magnesium gives one electron to each of

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:04.472
two chlorines.

00:04:05.193 --> 00:04:07.256
That's why the formula is M g C

00:04:07.477 --> 00:04:10.401
l two, and the diagram shows one magnesium

00:04:10.601 --> 00:04:13.125
ion, and two separate chloride ions.

00:04:14.188 --> 00:04:16.932
In a covalent Lewis diagram, the shared pair

00:04:16.993 --> 00:04:19.517
sits between the two atoms, and it's often

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:20.479
drawn as a line.

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:23.598
Pairs that aren't shared are called lone pairs.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:25.538
Water is a good example.

00:04:26.326 --> 00:04:29.461
Oxygen has six outer electrons, and each hydrogen

00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:30.044
has one.

00:04:30.848 --> 00:04:33.878
Oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen, so

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:36.426
it has two bonding pairs, and two lone

00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:36.727
pairs.

00:04:37.351 --> 00:04:39.488
That gives oxygen eight electrons around it.

00:04:40.273 --> 00:04:43.041
Each hydrogen has two, which is all hydrogen

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:43.382
needs.

00:04:44.331 --> 00:04:46.084
Sometimes atoms share two pairs.

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:49.817
In carbon dioxide, carbon has four outer electrons,

00:04:50.159 --> 00:04:51.602
and each oxygen has six.

00:04:52.345 --> 00:04:55.434
Carbon shares two pairs with each oxygen, making

00:04:55.556 --> 00:04:56.417
two double bonds.

00:04:57.121 --> 00:04:59.600
Now every atom has eight electrons around it.

00:05:00.237 --> 00:05:01.589
So here's the connection.

00:05:02.276 --> 00:05:05.124
Ionic and covalent bonding are two ends of

00:05:05.165 --> 00:05:06.387
the same tug of war.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.497
The bigger the electronegativity difference, the more one-sided

00:05:10.538 --> 00:05:10.978
the bond,

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.947
from sharing equally, to sharing unequally, to handing

00:05:14.988 --> 00:05:15.869
the electron over.

00:05:16.656 --> 00:05:17.867
Small gap, share.

00:05:18.475 --> 00:05:18.899
Big gap,

00:05:19.405 --> 00:05:19.809
transfer.

00:05:22.483 --> 00:05:22.867
Your turn.

00:05:23.612 --> 00:05:27.238
Using the electronegativity values given, classify the bonds

00:05:27.398 --> 00:05:31.565
potassium to chlorine, nitrogen to hydrogen, and bromine

00:05:31.685 --> 00:05:35.852
to bromine, as non-polar covalent, polar covalent or

00:05:36.013 --> 00:05:36.333
ionic.

00:05:37.035 --> 00:05:39.184
For any polar bond, show which atom is

00:05:39.264 --> 00:05:40.087
delta minus.

00:05:40.811 --> 00:05:41.758
Pause here to answer.

00:05:52.435 --> 00:05:55.341
Potassium and chlorine differ by two point four,

00:05:55.582 --> 00:05:57.326
so that bond is ionic.

00:05:58.088 --> 00:06:00.749
Nitrogen and hydrogen differ by zero point eight,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.871
so it's polar covalent, with delta minus on

00:06:03.912 --> 00:06:05.992
the nitrogen, because nitrogen pulls harder.

00:06:06.754 --> 00:06:09.622
Two bromines differ by zero, so that bond

00:06:09.763 --> 00:06:11.206
is non-polar covalent.

00:06:12.253 --> 00:06:14.486
Putting delta minus on the hydrogen is the

00:06:14.527 --> 00:06:14.788
slip.

00:06:15.353 --> 00:06:17.949
Delta minus always goes on the more electronegative

00:06:18.091 --> 00:06:18.332
atom.

00:06:19.958 --> 00:06:20.401
Last one.

00:06:20.846 --> 00:06:24.191
Draw Lewis dot diagrams for sodium oxide, showing

00:06:24.213 --> 00:06:27.097
the transfer of electrons, and for ammonia, N

00:06:27.258 --> 00:06:27.578
H three.

00:06:28.282 --> 00:06:29.169
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.101 --> 00:06:43.688
For sodium oxide, oxygen has six outer electrons,

00:06:44.009 --> 00:06:44.811
and needs two more.

00:06:45.573 --> 00:06:46.748
Each sodium gives one.

00:06:47.313 --> 00:06:49.719
So there are two sodium ions in brackets,

00:06:49.899 --> 00:06:50.882
each plus one,

00:06:51.383 --> 00:06:53.850
and one oxide ion with eight dots,

00:06:54.371 --> 00:06:55.775
and a charge of minus two.

00:06:56.577 --> 00:06:59.866
For ammonia, nitrogen has five outer electrons.

00:07:00.448 --> 00:07:02.432
It shares one pair with each of three

00:07:02.514 --> 00:07:04.799
hydrogens, which leaves one lone pair.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.007
Drawing sodium and oxygen sharing a pair is

00:07:08.048 --> 00:07:08.409
the trap.

00:07:09.011 --> 00:07:11.728
A big electronegativity difference means transfer.

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:14.174
One last thing.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.846
In a real crystal of salt, each sodium

00:07:17.006 --> 00:07:19.252
ion doesn't pair up with just one chloride.

00:07:19.813 --> 00:07:22.718
It attracts every chloride ion around it, and

00:07:22.778 --> 00:07:25.643
each chloride attracts the sodiums around it, building

00:07:25.663 --> 00:07:27.145
a huge, regular pattern.

00:07:27.827 --> 00:07:29.751
So why is that crystal so hard, and

00:07:29.791 --> 00:07:31.895
why does it carry electricity only once it

00:07:31.955 --> 00:07:32.917
melts or dissolves?

00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:34.246
That's next time.

00:07:36.928 --> 00:07:37.750
Here are the key notes.

00:07:38.274 --> 00:07:39.821
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:39.862 --> 00:07:40.283
to copy them.
